+    <section class="journal" id="roasters-journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <div>
+          <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+          <p class="byline">Notes on how our beans find their way from farm to cup</p>
+        </div>
+        <button type="button" class="summarize-btn" id="summarize-btn" aria-controls="journal-summary">
+          ✨ Summarize
+        </button>
+      </div>
+
+      <div class="summary" id="journal-summary" hidden aria-live="polite">
+        <h3>The Short Version</h3>
+        <p class="summary-status" id="summary-status"></p>
+        <ul id="summary-list"></ul>
+      </div>
+
+      <article class="journal-body" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag of coffee we roast starts with a relationship, not a spreadsheet. Long before a single bean
+          reaches our drum roaster, we've spent months talking with the people who grow it. Our sourcing team travels
+          to origin at least twice a year, visiting smallholder farms and cooperatives in Ethiopia, Colombia,
+          Guatemala, and Sumatra. We walk the rows, taste cherries straight off the branch, and sit down with
+          producers to understand what a good harvest means for their families and communities.</p>
+
+        <h3>Choosing Our Partners</h3>
+        <p>We look for farms that share our obsession with quality and our commitment to doing things the right way.
+          That means shade-grown coffee that protects native bird habitats, careful water management at the
+          washing stations, and growers who are willing to experiment with fermentation and drying methods to coax
+          out something special. Many of our partners are small family operations of fewer than five hectares, and
+          we've worked with some of them for more than a decade.</p>
+
+        <p>Rather than buying on the commodity market, we purchase directly or through a small number of trusted
+          importers who share full transparency on the supply chain. We pay well above Fairtrade minimums &mdash;
+          typically two to three times the commodity price &mdash; and we publish what we paid for every lot on our
+          website. When a farm invests in better drying beds or a new depulper, we often help fund it through
+          pre-harvest financing, so producers aren't forced to take on high-interest loans.</p>
+
+        <h3>Cupping and Selection</h3>
+        <p>Once samples arrive at our roastery, the real tasting begins. Our team cups every lot blind, scoring
+          aroma, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish. Only coffees scoring 85 points or higher on the Specialty
+          Coffee Association scale make it into our lineup. In a typical year we cup more than four hundred samples
+          and select fewer than thirty. It's a demanding process, but it's the only way we know to guarantee that
+          what's in your cup is genuinely exceptional.</p>
+
+        <p>We also pay close attention to how a coffee changes over time. Green coffee is a living agricultural
+          product, and its flavor can fade if it's stored poorly. That's why we ship in GrainPro-lined bags,
+          keep our warehouse climate-controlled, and plan our purchases so that every bean is roasted within nine
+          months of harvest.</p>
+
+        <h3>From Roaster to Your Cup</h3>
+        <p>We roast in small batches of no more than twelve kilograms, adjusting each profile to highlight the unique
+          character of the origin &mdash; bright and floral for a washed Ethiopian, rich and chocolatey for a
+          Sumatran wet-hulled lot. Every batch is logged and cupped again the next morning before it's approved for
+          sale. Coffee leaves our roastery within forty-eight hours of roasting, so it arrives at our cafés and your
+          doorstep at peak freshness.</p>
+
+        <p>Sourcing this way takes more time, more travel, and more money than the alternative. But we believe great
+          coffee should be good for everyone who touches it &mdash; from the farmer picking ripe cherries at dawn to
+          you, taking that first sip on a quiet morning. That's the promise behind every cup at The Daily Grind.</p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
